    "chaosLab": {
     "title": {
      "id": "Pendapatan ongkir yang 49% terlalu tinggi",
      "en": "The shipping income that was 49% too high"
     },
     "scenario": {
      "id": "Tim keuangan bertanya mengapa dashboard menyebut KopiKu memperoleh Rp 350.000 dari ongkir pesanan terkirim di bulan Januari, sementara laporan penyedia pembayaran menyebut Rp 235.000. Query dashboard ditulis berbulan-bulan lalu dan berjalan tiap hari tanpa error apa pun. Tidak ada yang mengubahnya. Analis yang menulisnya sudah keluar.\n\nQuery itu menggabungkan `orders` dengan `order_items` karena filter produk ditambahkan kuartal lalu (\"hanya hitung pesanan yang memuat biji Gayo\"), dan menjumlahkan `shipping_fee_idr` di seluruh baris hasil join.",
      "en": "The finance team asks why the dashboard says KopiKu earned Rp 350,000 in shipping fees on delivered orders in January, while the payment provider's report says Rp 235,000. The dashboard query was written months ago and has run every day without any error. Nobody has changed it. The analyst who wrote it has left.\n\nThe query joins `orders` to `order_items` because a filter on product was added last quarter (\"only count orders that contain Gayo beans\"), and sums `shipping_fee_idr` across the joined rows."
     },
     "breakageReason": {
      "id": "`shipping_fee_idr` disimpan sekali per **pesanan**, tetapi setelah join ke `order_items` ada satu baris per **baris produk**. Pesanan dengan tiga baris produk biaya kirimnya terhitung tiga kali. Hasilnya salah tepat sebanding dengan banyaknya pesanan multi-baris, sehingga tampak masuk akal dan melebar seiring waktu ketika pelanggan mulai membeli lebih banyak produk per pesanan.\n\nTidak ada error karena SQL tidak punya cara untuk tahu bahwa jumlah itu dimaksudkan per pesanan: query valid, hanya maknanya yang salah.",
      "en": "`shipping_fee_idr` is stored once per **order**, but after joining to `order_items` there is one row per order **line**. An order with three lines has its fee counted three times. The result is wrong exactly in proportion to how many multi-line orders there are, so it looked plausible and drifted over time as customers started buying more products per order.\n\nNo error is raised because SQL has no way to know the sum is meant to be per order: the query is valid, only its meaning is wrong."
     },
     "fixGuide": {
      "id": "1. Tentukan grain yang Anda inginkan: ongkir per pesanan. Agregasikan atau filter pada grain itu: pakai `WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM order_items i WHERE i.order_id = o.order_id AND i.product_id = 1)` alih-alih join, sehingga setiap pesanan tetap satu baris.\n2. Atau hitung tabel grain lebih halus dulu di CTE (pesanan distinct yang memuat produk itu) dan join *itu* ke `orders`.\n3. Tambahkan tes rekonsiliasi: total ongkir di dashboard harus sama dengan `SELECT SUM(shipping_fee_idr) FROM orders WHERE ...` dan idealnya cocok dengan laporan penyedia pembayaran dalam toleransi.\n4. Tambahkan pemeriksaan jumlah baris setelah join pada apa pun yang menjumlahkan nilai per pesanan.",
      "en": "1. Decide the grain you want: shipping fee per order. Aggregate or filter at that grain: use `WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM order_items i WHERE i.order_id = o.order_id AND i.product_id = 1)` instead of joining, so each order stays one row.\n2. Alternatively compute the finer-grain table first in a CTE (distinct orders containing the product) and join *that* to `orders`.\n3. Add a reconciliation test: the dashboard's shipping total must equal `SELECT SUM(shipping_fee_idr) FROM orders WHERE ...` and ideally match the payment provider's report within a tolerance.\n4. Add a row-count check after joins in anything that sums per-order values."
     },
     "defensivePattern": {
      "id": "Ketahui grain setiap tabel yang Anda join, dan jumlahkan nilai hanya pada grain tempat ia berada. Setelah join apa pun, tanyakan: apakah jumlah baris berubah dengan cara yang bisa saya jelaskan?",
      "en": "Know the grain of every table you join, and sum a value only at the grain where it lives. After any join, ask: did the row count change in a way I can explain?"
     }
    },
