     {
      "title": {
       "id": "Semantik waktu: Event time, processing time & aliran data tidak berurutan",
       "en": "Time semantics: Event time, processing time & out-of-order streams"
      },
      "body": {
       "id": "Dalam pemrosesan batch tradisional, waktu bersifat statis: file memuat data historis kemarin, dan Anda menjalankan kueri SQL atas seluruh snapshot tersebut. Dalam pemrosesan stream, waktu bersifat mengalir dan memiliki banyak dimensi. Terdapat tiga konsep waktu yang fundamental:\n\n```\n[Kejadian di Kendaraan Armada] ───> [Gateway Ingestion Jaringan] ───> [Engine Stream Processor]\n          Event Time                          Ingestion Time                      Processing Time\n     (t = 1704067250 UTC)                  (t = 1704067275 UTC)                 (t = 1704067282 UTC)\n```\n\n1. **Event Time:** Saat fisik ketika kejadian nyata berlangsung di lapangan, dicatat oleh jam sensor atau perangkat klien (contohnya saat penumpang menempelkan kartu pembayaran di armada `taxi_12`).\n2. **Ingestion Time:** Saat ketika paket data menembus firewall backend dan dicatat ke dalam log terdistribusi (misalnya partisi Kafka atau Redpanda).\n3. **Processing Time:** Waktu lokal jam dinding (wall-clock) dari node komputasi yang sedang mengeksekusi kode agregasi window.\n\n### Jebakan Penggunaan Processing Time:\nMengapa kita tidak menyederhanakan arsitektur dengan hanya memakai processing time? Cara tersebut memang tidak membutuhkan watermark, tidak perlu melacak late data, dan bebas buffer memori.\nNamun, bayangkan skenario armada kendaraan yang melintasi terowongan bawah tanah kota tanpa sinyal seluler:\n- Sepuluh taksi menyelesaikan perjalanan penumpang antara pukul 08:00 dan 08:05.\n- Karena berada di terowongan, modem seluler tidak dapat memancarkan paket data.\n- Pada pukul 08:15, seluruh armada keluar dari terowongan dan kembali memperoleh sinyal 5G.\n- Buffer telemetri lokal langsung mengirimkan 1.000 event secara serentak ke gateway dalam rentang dua detik.\n\nJika Anda mengelompokkan data berdasarkan **processing time**, pipeline Anda akan mencatat 0 perjalanan pada pukul 08:00-08:05, lalu mencatat lonjakan ekstrem sebesar 1.000 perjalanan pada pukul 08:15-08:16! Algoritma penetapan harga dinamis (surge pricing) akan menyimpulkan terjadi lonjakan permintaan darurat di terowongan pada 08:15 dan menaikkan tarif penumpang berkali-kali lipat, sementara menganggap pukul 08:00 tidak ada aktivitas pelanggan sama sekali.\n\nHanya **event-time windowing** yang mampu menyajikan kebenaran analitis. Tidak peduli kapan data tiba di server pengolah, event dari 08:02:30 harus secara akurat dimasukkan ke dalam window `[08:00:00, 08:05:00)`.\n\n### Mengenal Dataset Seed:\n**Catatan jujur: aliran ini sintetis.** `fetch_data.py` membuatnya dari random seed tetap untuk meniru kendaraan dengan pengiriman tertunda dan tidak berurutan; ini bukan telemetri taksi sungguhan (untuk data nyata, lihat Modul 4.1 dan 4.2). Yang nyata adalah masalahnya: setiap angka di bawah ini diukur pada 1.000 event tersebut.\n\nDataset seed kita (`labs/mod-4-3/data/events_seed.jsonl`) berisi 1.000 event telemetri armada deterministik dari 50 kendaraan di berbagai zona taksi perkotaan:\n```json\n{\n  \"event_id\": \"evt_0001\",\n  \"vehicle_id\": \"taxi_29\",\n  \"event_time\": 1704067266,\n  \"zone_id\": 186,\n  \"fare_amount\": 28.53,\n  \"trip_distance\": 5.48,\n  \"passenger_count\": 1\n}\n```\nKarena perangkat seluler mengalami fluktuasi latensi jaringan (network jitter), **346 event dalam dataset ini tiba tidak berurutan** (di mana `event_time < previous_event_time`). Engine streaming Anda harus menata keteraturan data ini secara matematis.\n\n> 💡 **Jeda Praktik 1 (Langkah 1: Profiling Stream Mentah & Pembuatan profile.json):**\n> 1. Buat file `profile.json` di folder workspace Anda (`~/fleet/profile.json`) menggunakan teks editor.\n> 2. Hitung 7 metrik statistik dari file `events_seed.jsonl`: `total_stream_events`, `min_event_time`, `max_event_time`, `distinct_vehicles`, `out_of_order_count`, `busiest_zone_id`, dan `busiest_zone_event_count`.\n> 3. Jalankan pemeriksaan langkah 1 di terminal:\n>    `python3 labs/mod-4-3/check.py ~/fleet --step 1`\n> 4. **Indikator Sukses:** Seluruh 7 metrik berstatus `PASS`, memvalidasi ekstraksi profiling event stream Anda cocok 100% dengan data acuan.",
       "en": "In batch processing, time is static: a file contains historical rows from yesterday, and you run a SQL query over the whole dataset. In stream processing, time is continuous and multifaceted. There are three distinct notions of time:\n\n```\n[Event Occurrence in Taxi] ─────> [Network Ingestion Gateway] ─────> [Stream Processor Engine]\n       Event Time                       Ingestion Time                     Processing Time\n (t = 1704067250 UTC)                (t = 1704067275 UTC)                (t = 1704067282 UTC)\n```\n\n1. **Event Time:** The instant at which the physical event occurred in the real world, captured by the sensor or client device clock (e.g., when a passenger tapped their credit card in taxi `taxi_12`).\n2. **Ingestion Time:** The instant at which the event crossed the firewall and was appended to the distributed messaging log (e.g., Kafka or Redpanda partition leader).\n3. **Processing Time:** The local wall-clock time of the worker node executing the window aggregation code.\n\n### The Processing Time Trap:\nWhy not simply use processing time? It requires no stateful watermarking, no late data handling, and zero buffer queues.\nConsider connected fleet vehicles driving through urban tunnels or cellular dead zones:\n- Ten taxis complete trips between 08:00 and 08:05.\n- Because they are inside an underground tunnel, their cellular modems cannot transmit packets.\n- At 08:15, all ten taxis emerge from the tunnel and establish 5G connectivity.\n- Their telemetry buffers dump 1,000 buffered events into the ingestion gateway simultaneously within two seconds.\n\nIf you group events by **processing time**, your pipeline will register 0 trips between 08:00 and 08:05, and a massive spike of 1,000 trips between 08:15 and 08:16! An automated surge-pricing system or fleet capacity allocator will conclude that an unprecedented emergency surge occurred at 08:15 and raise passenger fares unfairly, while assuming 08:00 had zero customer demand.\n\nOnly **event-time windowing** provides truthful analytical metrics. Regardless of when the data arrives at the server, an event from 08:02:30 must be assigned to the `[08:00:00, 08:05:00)` window.\n\n### Meet the Seed Dataset:\n**Honest note: this stream is synthetic.** `fetch_data.py` generates it from a fixed random seed to imitate vehicles with delayed, out-of-order delivery; it is not real taxi telemetry (for real data, see Module 4.1 and 4.2). What is real is the problem: every number below was measured on these 1,000 events.\n\nOur seed stream (`labs/mod-4-3/data/events_seed.jsonl`) consists of 1,000 deterministic fleet telemetry events spanning 50 vehicles across dense urban taxi zones:\n```json\n{\n  \"event_id\": \"evt_0001\",\n  \"vehicle_id\": \"taxi_29\",\n  \"event_time\": 1704067266,\n  \"zone_id\": 186,\n  \"fare_amount\": 28.53,\n  \"trip_distance\": 5.48,\n  \"passenger_count\": 1\n}\n```\nBecause mobile devices experience variable network jitter, **346 events in this stream arrive out of order** (where `event_time < previous_event_time`). Your processor must restore order mathematically.\n\n> 💡 **Practice Pause 1 (Step 1: Raw Stream Profiling & profile.json Creation):**\n> 1. Create `profile.json` in your workspace folder (`~/fleet/profile.json`) using your text editor.\n> 2. Compute the 7 statistical metrics from `events_seed.jsonl`: `total_stream_events`, `min_event_time`, `max_event_time`, `distinct_vehicles`, `out_of_order_count`, `busiest_zone_id`, and `busiest_zone_event_count`.\n> 3. Run the step 1 check in terminal:\n>    `python3 labs/mod-4-3/check.py ~/fleet --step 1`\n> 4. **Success Indicator:** All 7 metrics report `PASS`, confirming your raw stream profiling matches reference truth."
      }
     },
     {
      "title": {
       "id": "Watermark, toleransi keterlambatan & mekanisme penggusuran window",
       "en": "Watermarks, allowed lateness & window eviction mechanics"
      },
      "body": {
       "id": "Jika kita mengelompokkan data berdasarkan event time, muncul dilema arsitektur kritis: **berapa lama engine streaming harus menunggu sebelum menyatakan sebuah window selesai dan memancarkan hasilnya?**\nJika modem kendaraan baru terhubung kembali tiga hari kemudian, apakah kita harus membiarkan seluruh window masa lalu tetap terbuka di memori? Tentu tidak. Mempertahankan jutaan window terbuka di memori RAM akan memicu crash Out Of Memory (OOM).\n\nDi sinilah konsep **watermark** hadir (pertama kali diformalkan oleh Tyler Akidau dkk. dalam model Google MillWheel dan Apache Beam).\n\n```\nEvent Stream:  [e1: t=100] ──> [e2: t=250] ──> [e3: t=190] ──> [e4: t=400]\n                                                                     │\n                                                 Delay = 120s        ▼\n                                          Watermark = 400 - 120 = 280\n                                          Window dengan end <= 280 DITUTUP!\n```\n\n### 1. Definisi Watermark:\nWatermark adalah metrik temporal yang monotonik dan tidak pernah mundur, yang mewakili tingkat kelengkapan aliran data. Ketika nilai watermark `W = t`, sistem menyatakan jaminan:\n`\"Kami memperkirakan tidak ada lagi event dengan event_time <= t yang akan tiba di masa mendatang.\"`\n\nDalam engine streaming kita, kita menerapkan **bounded out-of-orderness watermark** dengan jeda toleransi keterlambatan (allowed lateness delay) sebesar `D`:\n`W_kandidat = t_event - D`\n`W = max(W_sebelumnya, W_kandidat)`\n\nNilai watermark **tidak boleh bergerak mundur**, bahkan ketika event yang out of order tiba dengan timestamp yang lebih tua dari timestamp terbaru yang pernah dicatat.\n\n### 2. Siklus Hidup dan Penggusuran Window (Eviction):\nSebuah window didefinisikan oleh interval setengah terbuka `[t_start, t_end)`.\n- **Window Terbuka:** Selama `t_end > W`, window berstatus terbuka di memori RAM. Setiap event yang berada di rentang waktu tersebut ditambahkan ke akumulator window.\n- **Penggusuran / Penutupan Window:** Begitu watermark bergerak maju hingga `t_end <= W`, window dinyatakan ditutup. Hasil agregasi final dipancarkan ke database analitik (DuckDB), dan akumulator di memori dihapus untuk menghemat RAM.\n\n### 3. Out-of-Order vs. Late Data:\nSangat penting membedakan **event yang out of order** dengan **event yang late (terlambat)**:\n- **Out-of-order event:** Event yang memiliki `t_event < t_terbaru_dilihat`, namun window tempatnya berada masih berstatus terbuka (`t_end > W`). Event ini **valid dan diterima**; metriknya langsung diakumulasikan ke window yang bersangkutan.\n- **Late event:** Event yang window tujuannya sudah ditutup dan digusur dari memori (`t_end <= W`). Agregasi window tersebut sudah terlanjur dipancarkan ke konsumen hilir. Memasukkannya kembali akan merusak keabsahan laporan keuangan yang sudah terbit, sehingga event ini **ditolak dan dikarantina**.\n- **Sliding window (keterbatasan yang diketahui):** sebuah event baru dikirim ke dead-letter hanya jika *semua* window-nya sudah tertutup. Jika sebagian tertutup dan sebagian masih terbuka, event memperbarui yang terbuka dan window yang sudah tertutup diam-diam tidak memilikinya. Engine sungguhan menghitung penjatuhan parsial seperti ini dalam sebuah metrik; ingat hal ini saat membandingkan hasil sliding dengan hitung ulang batch.\n\n> 💡 **Jeda Praktik 2 (Langkah 2: Kontrak Tumbling Event-Time Window & Penyimpanan DuckDB):**\n> 1. Buka file `stream_processor.py` di workspace Anda (`~/fleet/stream_processor.py`) menggunakan teks editor.\n> 2. Implementasikan argumen CLI (`--input-stream`, `--output-dir`, `--state-file`, `--window-size`, `--watermark-delay`), kalkulasi tumbling window (`start = floor(ts / size) * size`), dan buat tabel `windowed_metrics` di DuckDB secara idempoten.\n> 3. Jalankan pemeriksaan langkah 2 di terminal:\n>    `python3 labs/mod-4-3/check.py ~/fleet --step 2`\n> 4. **Indikator Sukses:** Seluruh pengujian langkah 2 berstatus `PASS`, memvalidasi skema DuckDB, pengelompokan tumbling window, dan pemancaran metrik agregasi.",
       "en": "If we aggregate by event time, a critical dilemma arises: **how long must the stream processor wait before declaring a window complete and emitting its metrics?**\nIf a taxi's cellular modem reconnects three days late, can we keep all past windows open indefinitely? No. Keeping millions of historical windows open in memory would exhaust RAM (Out Of Memory crash).\n\nEnter **watermarks** (conceptualized by Tyler Akidau et al. in the Google MillWheel and Apache Beam models).\n\n```\nEvent Stream:  [e1: t=100] ──> [e2: t=250] ──> [e3: t=190] ──> [e4: t=400]\n                                                                     │\n                                                 Delay = 120s        ▼\n                                          Watermark = 400 - 120 = 280\n                                          Windows with end <= 280 are CLOSED!\n```\n\n### 1. Watermark Definition:\nA watermark is a monotonically non-decreasing metric timestamp representing temporal completeness. When watermark `W = t`, the streaming engine asserts:\n`\"We expect no further events with event_time <= t to arrive.\"`\n\nIn our streaming engine, we implement **bounded out-of-orderness watermarks** with an allowed lateness delay `D`:\n`W_candidate = t_event - D`\n`W = max(W_previous, W_candidate)`\n\nWatermarks must **never move backward**, even when an out-of-order event arrives with a timestamp smaller than the newest event seen.\n\n### 2. Window Lifecycles and Eviction:\nA window is defined by a half-open temporal interval `[t_start, t_end)`.\n- **Open Window:** While `t_end > W`, the window remains open in memory. Any event falling into this interval is aggregated into the window accumulator.\n- **Eviction / Closure:** When watermark advances such that `t_end <= W`, the window is closed. Its final aggregates are emitted to analytical storage (DuckDB), and its in-memory accumulator is evicted to reclaim RAM.\n\n### 3. Out-of-Order vs. Late Data:\nIt is crucial to distinguish **out-of-order events** from **late events**:\n- **Out-of-order event:** An event whose `t_event < t_newest_seen`, but whose candidate window has not closed yet (`t_end > W`). This event is **valid and welcomed**; it is folded into the open window accumulator.\n- **Late event:** An event whose candidate window has already been closed and evicted (`t_end <= W`). The window aggregate was already emitted and published to downstream consumers. The event cannot be included without rewriting published history, so it is **dropped and quarantined**.\n- **Sliding windows (a known limitation):** an event is dead-lettered only when *all* of its windows are closed. If some are closed and some still open, the event updates the open ones and the closed windows silently miss it. Real engines count such partial drops in a metric; keep that in mind when you compare sliding results with a batch recount.\n\n> 💡 **Practice Pause 2 (Step 2: Baseline Tumbling Event-Time Windows & DuckDB Storage):**\n> 1. Open `stream_processor.py` in your workspace (`~/fleet/stream_processor.py`) using your text editor.\n> 2. Implement CLI arguments (`--input-stream`, `--output-dir`, `--state-file`, `--window-size`, `--watermark-delay`), tumbling window calculation (`start = floor(ts / size) * size`), and create table `windowed_metrics` in DuckDB idempotently.\n> 3. Run the step 2 check in terminal:\n>    `python3 labs/mod-4-3/check.py ~/fleet --step 2`\n> 4. **Success Indicator:** All step 2 checks report `PASS`, validating DuckDB schema, tumbling window grouping, and aggregate metric emission."
      }
     },
     {
      "title": {
       "id": "Consumer offset, checkpointing & idempotensi analitik",
       "en": "Consumer offsets, checkpointing & analytical idempotency"
      },
      "body": {
       "id": "Engine streaming beroperasi terus-menerus tanpa henti. Gangguan perangkat keras, restart kontainer, dan pembaruan sistem operasi adalah keniscayaan di lingkungan produksi. Engine yang tangguh harus mampu bertahan dari crash dengan semantik **exactly-once processing** di lapisan database.\n\n```\nAliran Data Masuk (JSONL / Kafka)\n           │\n           ▼\n┌───────────────────────┐\n│ Stream Processor      │ ──[Simpan Checkpoint]──> offsets.json.tmp ──> [os.replace] ──> offsets.json\n│ • Membaca baris N     │\n│ • Update akumulator   │\n└──────────┬────────────┘\n           │\n           ▼ [Pancarkan Window via Tabel Staging Sementara]\n┌───────────────────────┐\n│ DuckDB Warehouse      │ (DELETE WHERE pk IN staging; INSERT SELECT * FROM staging;)\n│ PRIMARY KEY:          │\n│ (start, end, zone_id) │\n└───────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### 1. Kontrak Checkpoint (`offsets.json`):\nUntuk melanjutkan pemrosesan tanpa harus mengulang pembacaan dari baris pertama, engine mencatat progres konsumsinya ke file checkpoint state:\n```json\n{\n  \"last_offset\": 200,\n  \"high_watermark\": 1704071400,\n  \"total_events_processed\": 200,\n  \"total_late_events_dropped\": 0,\n  \"total_windows_emitted\": 118,\n  \"open_windows\": {\n    \"1704071100_1704071400_186\": {\n      \"trips\": 4,\n      \"passengers\": 7,\n      \"total_fare\": 112.50\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Aturan Pelestarian Akumulator In-Flight:\nKesalahan fatal yang sering dilakukan pemula adalah *hanya* menyimpan angka offset (`last_offset: 200`) dan melupakan akumulator window yang sedang berjalan di memori.\nJika proses terhenti saat window 5 menit baru terisi separuh, proses yang dimulai ulang dengan memori kosong akan memecah window tersebut menjadi dua fragmen: perjalanan sebelum crash dipancarkan dengan angka yang belum lengkap, dan perjalanan pasca-crash membentuk window terpisah yang cacat!\nUntuk menjamin kontinuitas stream yang sempurna, checkpoint state wajib menyimpan angka offset **sekaligus data akumulator window yang masih terbuka (`open_windows`)**. Saat restart, akumulator terbuka dipulihkan kembali ke memori secara utuh.\n\n### 3. Commit State Atomik via Rename POSIX:\nMenulis langsung ke file `offsets.json` sangat berbahaya jika sistem operasi mati mendadak saat operasi tulis berlangsung:\n```python\ntmp_file = state_path.with_suffix(state_path.suffix + \".tmp\")\ntmp_file.write_text(json.dumps(state_data, indent=2))\ntmp_file.replace(state_path)  # Atomik pada filesystem POSIX via syscall rename(2)\n```\nProses lain atau prosedur pemulihan crash akan selalu membaca file JSON lama yang valid atau file JSON baru yang lengkap, tidak akan pernah menemukan file dengan isi terpotong separuh.\n\n### 4. Idempotensi Penyimpanan di DuckDB:\nKetika engine pulih dari kegagalan, sistem biasanya membaca ulang beberapa event dari titik checkpoint terakhir. Jika operasi penulisan database adalah `INSERT` biasa, baris ganda akan merusak metrik analitik.\nKita menegakkan idempotensi di DuckDB menggunakan primary key komposit dan mekanisme staging:\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS windowed_metrics (\n    window_start TIMESTAMP,\n    window_end TIMESTAMP,\n    zone_id INTEGER,\n    total_trips BIGINT,\n    total_passengers BIGINT,\n    total_fare DOUBLE,\n    avg_fare DOUBLE,\n    emitted_at TIMESTAMP,\n    PRIMARY KEY (window_start, window_end, zone_id)\n);\n```\nSaat pemancaran window, baris dimasukkan ke tabel sementara, baris lama di tabel utama yang memiliki kecocokan `(window_start, window_end, zone_id)` dihapus, dan metrik terbaru disisipkan secara bersih.\n\n> 💡 **Jeda Praktik 3 (Langkah 3: Watermark Monotonik & Karantina DLQ Late Data):**\n> 1. Buka file `stream_processor.py` di workspace Anda menggunakan teks editor.\n> 2. Terapkan bounded watermark monotonik (`W = max(W, ts - delay)`), gusur dan pancarkan window saat `window_end <= W`, dan salurkan event terlambat ke `<output-dir>/dlq/late_events.jsonl` dengan `error_code: \"LATE_EVENT\"`.\n> 3. Jalankan pemeriksaan langkah 3 di terminal:\n>    `python3 labs/mod-4-3/check.py ~/fleet --step 3`\n> 4. **Indikator Sukses:** Seluruh pengujian langkah 3 berstatus `PASS`, membuktikan penanganan event out-of-order berjalan benar dan event late dikarantina tanpa merusak metrik window.",
       "en": "Streaming engines run continuously for months. Hardware failures, operating system kernel updates, and container restarts are guaranteed occurrences. A robust engine must survive crashes with **exactly-once processing semantics** at the storage layer.\n\n```\nIncoming Stream (JSONL / Kafka)\n           │\n           ▼\n┌───────────────────────┐\n│ Stream Processor      │ ──[Save Checkpoint]──> offsets.json.tmp ──> [os.replace] ──> offsets.json\n│ • Reads line N        │\n│ • Updates accumulators│\n└──────────┬────────────┘\n           │\n           ▼ [Emit Window via Temp Staging Table]\n┌───────────────────────┐\n│ DuckDB Warehouse      │ (DELETE WHERE pk IN staging; INSERT SELECT * FROM staging;)\n│ PRIMARY KEY:          │\n│ (start, end, zone_id) │\n└───────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### 1. The Checkpoint Contract (`offsets.json`):\nTo resume processing without reprocessing the entire stream from scratch, the processor tracks its consumption progress in a checkpoint state file:\n```json\n{\n  \"last_offset\": 200,\n  \"high_watermark\": 1704071400,\n  \"total_events_processed\": 200,\n  \"total_late_events_dropped\": 0,\n  \"total_windows_emitted\": 118,\n  \"open_windows\": {\n    \"1704071100_1704071400_186\": {\n      \"trips\": 4,\n      \"passengers\": 7,\n      \"total_fare\": 112.50\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. The In-Flight Accumulator Preservation Rule:\nA common architectural error in novice streaming systems is checkpointing *only* the offset integer (`last_offset: 200`) while ignoring in-memory window accumulators.\nIf the processor terminates while a 5-minute window is halfway through accumulating, restarting with an empty in-memory state will split the window in two: the pre-crash trips were emitted with incomplete counts, and post-crash trips form a separate incomplete window!\nTo guarantee exact stream continuity, a state checkpoint must serialize both the stream offset **and the open window accumulators (`open_windows`)**. On restart, open accumulators are restored into memory seamlessly.\n\n### 3. Atomic State Commit via POSIX Rename:\nWriting directly to `offsets.json` risks corrupting the file if the operating system crashes mid-write:\n```python\ntmp_file = state_path.with_suffix(state_path.suffix + \".tmp\")\ntmp_file.write_text(json.dumps(state_data, indent=2))\ntmp_file.replace(state_path)  # Atomic on POSIX filesystems via rename(2)\n```\nAny concurrent reader or recovering process always sees either the complete prior checkpoint or the complete new checkpoint, never a half-flushed buffer.\n\n### 4. Idempotent Storage Emission in DuckDB:\nWhen an engine crashes and recovers, it may replay events from the last committed checkpoint. If your database write is a naive `INSERT`, duplicate rows will pollute analytical metrics.\nWe enforce idempotency in DuckDB using composite primary keys and atomic staging swaps:\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS windowed_metrics (\n    window_start TIMESTAMP,\n    window_end TIMESTAMP,\n    zone_id INTEGER,\n    total_trips BIGINT,\n    total_passengers BIGINT,\n    total_fare DOUBLE,\n    avg_fare DOUBLE,\n    emitted_at TIMESTAMP,\n    PRIMARY KEY (window_start, window_end, zone_id)\n);\n```\nDuring emission, rows are inserted into a temporary staging table, existing rows matching `(window_start, window_end, zone_id)` are deleted in DuckDB, and the latest aggregated metrics are inserted cleanly.\n\n> 💡 **Practice Pause 3 (Step 3: Monotonic Watermark & Late Data DLQ Quarantine):**\n> 1. Open `stream_processor.py` in your workspace using your text editor.\n> 2. Implement monotonic bounded watermark (`W = max(W, ts - delay)`), evict and emit windows when `window_end <= W`, and route late events to `<output-dir>/dlq/late_events.jsonl` with `error_code: \"LATE_EVENT\"`.\n> 3. Run the step 3 check in terminal:\n>    `python3 labs/mod-4-3/check.py ~/fleet --step 3`\n> 4. **Success Indicator:** All step 3 checks report `PASS`, proving out-of-order events are absorbed correctly and late events are quarantined cleanly."
      }
     },
     {
      "title": {
       "id": "Tumbling vs. sliding window dan Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ)",
       "en": "Tumbling vs. sliding windows and Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ)"
      },
      "body": {
       "id": "Arsitektur streaming real-time menggunakan beberapa pola windowing bergantung pada kebutuhan analitik bisnis.\n\n```\nTumbling Window (Size = 300s, Slide = 300s): Blok tetap, berdampingan, tanpa tumpang-tindih\n[00:00 - 05:00)        [05:00 - 10:00)        [10:00 - 15:00)\n\nSliding Window (Size = 300s, Slide = 60s): Blok bergerak, saling tumpang-tindih\n[00:00 - 05:00)\n   [01:00 - 06:00)\n      [02:00 - 07:00)\n         [03:00 - 08:00)\n            [04:00 - 09:00)\n```\n\n### 1. Tumbling Window:\nTumbling window mempartisi aliran waktu menjadi blok-blok tetap yang tidak saling tumpang-tindih.\nUntuk ukuran window `L`, sebuah event pada timestamp `t` dimasukkan tepat ke **satu** window yang dimulai pada:\n`t_start = floor(t / L) * L`\n`t_end = t_start + L`\nTumbling window sangat ideal untuk penyelesaian transaksi finansial periodik (misalnya rekonsiliasi pendapatan per jam, rekap komisi harian pengemudi).\n\n### 2. Sliding Window:\nSliding window menghasilkan metrik yang diperhalus secara kontinu seiring berjalannya waktu (misalnya rata-rata kecepatan armada 5 menit terakhir yang diperbarui setiap 60 detik).\nDengan durasi window `L` dan interval pergeseran `S` (di mana `S < L`):\nSebuah event pada timestamp `t` berada di beberapa window tumpang-tindih yang dimulai pada kelipatan bulat `k * S`:\n`k * S <= t < k * S + L`\nMaka batas indeks bilangan bulat `k` adalah:\n`t - L < k * S <= t`, sehingga `(t - L) / S < k <= t / S`\nRentang nilai integer `k` bergerak dari:\n`k_min = floor((t - L + S) / S)` hingga `k_max = floor(t / S)`\nUntuk `L = 300 detik` dan `S = 60 detik`, setiap event masuk ke tepat `(300 / 60) = 5` window yang saling tumpang-tindih! Logika akumulator Anda harus memperbarui kelima bucket tersebut secara simultan.\n\n### 3. Karantina Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ):\nKetika event tiba di mana seluruh window kandidatnya sudah digusur (`t_end <= W`), apa yang harus dilakukan?\nMembuang data secara diam-diam menghilangkan jejak audit keuangan; memasukkannya ke window tertutup merusak integritas laporan analitik yang sudah diterbitkan.\nSolusi standar industri adalah **Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ)**. Event yang terlambat dicatat ke dalam `<output-dir>/dlq/late_events.jsonl` beserta metadata audit:\n```json\n{\n  \"error_code\": \"LATE_EVENT\",\n  \"event_id\": \"evt_0842\",\n  \"event_time\": 1704067280,\n  \"current_watermark\": 1704067830,\n  \"dropped_at\": \"2024-01-08T12:00:00Z\",\n  \"raw_payload\": { ... }\n}\n```\nTim keandalan sistem (SRE) dapat memantau laju data yang masuk ke DLQ, memicu peringatan jika menara seluler tertentu mengalami keterlambatan paket parah, dan memproses ulang data tersebut di pipeline rekonsiliasi batch offline.\n\n> 💡 **Jeda Praktik 4 (Langkah 4: Checkpointing Consumer Offset & Crash Recovery):**\n> 1. Buka file `stream_processor.py` di workspace Anda menggunakan teks editor.\n> 2. Simpan `last_offset` dan akumulator in-flight `open_windows` secara atomik (`.tmp` + `os.replace`), serta baca offset pada awal eksekusi untuk melewati event yang sudah terproses.\n> 3. Jalankan pemeriksaan langkah 4 di terminal:\n>    `python3 labs/mod-4-3/check.py ~/fleet --step 4`\n> 4. **Indikator Sukses:** Seluruh pengujian langkah 4 berstatus `PASS`, memverifikasi bahwa crash mendadak di tengah pemrosesan dapat dipulihkan tanpa duplikasi dan tanpa kehilangan event.",
       "en": "Real-time streaming architectures employ multiple windowing patterns depending on business requirements.\n\n```\nTumbling Windows (Size = 300s, Slide = 300s): Non-overlapping, contiguous fixed blocks\n[00:00 - 05:00)        [05:00 - 10:00)        [10:00 - 15:00)\n\nSliding Windows (Size = 300s, Slide = 60s): Overlapping moving blocks\n[00:00 - 05:00)\n   [01:00 - 06:00)\n      [02:00 - 07:00)\n         [03:00 - 08:00)\n            [04:00 - 09:00)\n```\n\n### 1. Tumbling Windows:\nTumbling windows partition time into non-overlapping, contiguous chunks.\nFor window size `L`, an event at timestamp `t` belongs to exactly **one** window starting at:\n`t_start = floor(t / L) * L`\n`t_end = t_start + L`\nTumbling windows are ideal for discrete periodic financial settlements (e.g., hourly revenue reconciliation, daily driver payouts).\n\n### 2. Sliding Windows:\nSliding windows provide a continuous smoothed metric over time (e.g., 5-minute moving average speed computed every 60 seconds).\nGiven window duration `L` and slide step `S` (where `S < L`):\nA single event at timestamp `t` falls into multiple overlapping windows starting at integer multiples `k * S` such that:\n`k * S <= t < k * S + L`\nSolving for integer `k`:\n`t - L < k * S <= t`, so `(t - L) / S < k <= t / S`\nThe discrete window indices `k` range from:\n`k_min = floor((t - L + S) / S)` to `k_max = floor(t / S)`\nFor `L = 300s` and `S = 60s`, an event falls into exactly `(300 / 60) = 5` overlapping windows! Your accumulator logic must update all 5 buckets simultaneously.\n\n### 3. Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) Quarantine:\nWhen an event arrives whose candidate windows have all been evicted (`t_end <= W`), what should happen?\nSilently discarding the event destroys financial traceability; allowing it into closed windows corrupts historical ledger reports.\nThe production solution is a **Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ)**. The dropped event is appended to `<output-dir>/dlq/late_events.jsonl` with audit metadata:\n```json\n{\n  \"error_code\": \"LATE_EVENT\",\n  \"event_id\": \"evt_0842\",\n  \"event_time\": 1704067280,\n  \"current_watermark\": 1704067830,\n  \"dropped_at\": \"2024-01-08T12:00:00Z\",\n  \"raw_payload\": { ... }\n}\n```\nSite reliability engineers can monitor DLQ arrival rates, alert when cellular towers cause excessive packet lag, and replay quarantined records into batch reconciliation pipelines.\n\n> 💡 **Practice Pause 4 (Step 4: Consumer Offset Checkpointing & Crash Recovery):**\n> 1. Open `stream_processor.py` in your workspace using your text editor.\n> 2. Persist `last_offset` and in-flight `open_windows` accumulators atomically (`.tmp` + `os.replace`), and read the offset upon startup to skip previously committed events.\n> 3. Run the step 4 check in terminal:\n>    `python3 labs/mod-4-3/check.py ~/fleet --step 4`\n> 4. **Success Indicator:** All step 4 checks report `PASS`, confirming mid-stream crash recovery resumes without duplicate records or data loss."
      }
     }
